# Jarvis Environment Variables
# Copy this to ~/jarvis/.env and fill in your values.
# This file is NOT committed to git.

# Home Assistant
HA_URL=http://192.168.68.113:8123
HA_TOKEN=your-home-assistant-long-lived-access-token-here

# n8n
N8N_URL=http://localhost:20003
N8N_API_KEY=your-n8n-api-key-here
# Calendar sync: scheduled/recurring reminders are mirrored to Google Calendar via
# the "JARVIS - Create Calendar Event" n8n webhook workflow (n8n/workflows/), which
# uses a Google *service account* (no OAuth/HTTPS redirect needed on a headless box).
# Setup: create a service account + JSON key in Google Cloud, enable the Calendar API,
# share your calendar with the service account email ("Make changes to events"), then
# bind the "Google Service Account" credential on the workflow's HTTP Request node.
# GCAL_CALENDAR_ID must be the calendar you shared (usually your Gmail address) —
# a service account's own "primary" is NOT your calendar.
GCAL_CALENDAR_ID=your-email@gmail.com
# Webhook base defaults to ${N8N_URL}/webhook if unset. CALENDAR_SYNC_ENABLED=false disables it.
# N8N_WEBHOOK_URL=http://localhost:20003/webhook
# N8N_WEBHOOK_SECRET=                  # optional shared secret (sent as x-jarvis-secret header)
# CALENDAR_SYNC_ENABLED=true

# Telegram (for monitoring alerts)
TG_BOT_TOKEN=your-telegram-bot-token-here
TG_CHAT_ID=your-telegram-chat-id-here

# qBittorrent
# qBittorrent WebUI (used by /download). URL host:port must match what the
# WebUI itself uses — the bot sends Referer/Origin for CSRF. Prefer localhost
# when the bot and qBT share the mini-pc.
QBT_URL=http://localhost:20008
QBT_USERNAME=admin
QBT_PASSWORD=your-qbittorrent-password-here

# Jellyfin (media library agent + /jellyfin command)
# JELLYFIN_URL defaults to http://localhost:20002 if unset.
# JELLYFIN_TOKEN: create an API key in Jellyfin Dashboard → API Keys.
# JELLYFIN_USER_ID: optional; if unset, the first (admin) user is used for
#   user-specific views (continue watching, recommendations, recently added).
JELLYFIN_URL=http://localhost:20002
JELLYFIN_TOKEN=your-jellyfin-api-key-here
# JELLYFIN_USER_ID=

# Long-term memory (PostgreSQL + PGVector)
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://jarvis:your-password@localhost:20004/jarvis
OPENAI_API_KEY=your-openai-api-key-here

# AI models — front layer uses the Haiku tier (cheap), falls back to GPT-4o-mini
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-anthropic-api-key-here

# Model registry (central — see telegram-bot/src/models.js).
# Every Claude model ID JARVIS uses resolves from here, so bumping a tier
# (e.g. Haiku 4.5 → 5) is a ONE-LINE change with no code edit. All optional;
# the defaults below are the current-generation IDs.
#   MODEL_HAIKU   fast/cheap: routing, classifiers, single-tool agents
#   MODEL_SONNET  balanced: research, complex MCP tool-chaining
#   MODEL_OPUS    heavy agentic: delegate, self-development, /deep
#   MODEL_FABLE   top tier (pricier/slower) — only used if a role points at it
#   MODEL_FRONT   the front router model (defaults to MODEL_HAIKU)
# MODEL_HAIKU=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001
# MODEL_SONNET=claude-sonnet-5
# MODEL_OPUS=claude-opus-5
# MODEL_FABLE=claude-fable-5
# MODEL_FRONT=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001
# Per-role overrides (default to the tier shown; e.g. point research at Fable):
# RESEARCH_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# MCP_AGENT_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001
# MCP_AGENT_MODEL_COMPLEX=claude-sonnet-5
# RESEARCH_DYNAMIC_FILTER: the deep-research agent uses dynamic filtering
#   (web_search_20260209 — Claude filters search results in a code sandbox
#   before they hit context, cheaper + sharper) on capable models. Set to false
#   to force the basic web_search tools (e.g. if RESEARCH_MODEL is an older model).
# RESEARCH_DYNAMIC_FILTER=true

# Headless brain HTTP endpoint
# The bot exposes the same brain the Telegram handler uses so other surfaces
# (HA Assist / Companion, a phone shortcut) can send text and get a reply.
# It is OPT-IN: the endpoint only starts if ASK_HTTP_TOKEN is set, and it can
# drive the whole house — keep the token secret.
#
# Endpoints:
#   GET  /health
#   POST /ask                      — JARVIS-native { "text": "..." }
#   GET  /v1/models                — OpenAI-compatible (HA setup discovery)
#   POST /v1/chat/completions      — OpenAI-compatible chat (HA OpenAI Conversation)
#
# ASK_HTTP_TOKEN: bearer token clients must present (Authorization: Bearer <token>).
# ASK_HTTP_PORT:  listen port (default 20010; 20007 is nginx HTTPS on this host).
# ASK_HTTP_BIND:  bind address (default 127.0.0.1).
#   For Home Assistant on another host (e.g. HA at 192.168.68.113), set:
#     ASK_HTTP_BIND=0.0.0.0
#   Extra endpoint used by away-mode motion alerts:
#     POST /cam/snapshot   — grab a ustreamer still and send it to Telegram
#   ustreamer webcam (LAN live feed for HA):
#     still:  http://192.168.68.124:20011/snapshot
#     stream: http://192.168.68.124:20011/stream
# WEBCAM_SNAPSHOT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:20011/snapshot
#   then point HA OpenAI Conversation at:
#     Base URL:  http://<mini-pc-lan-ip>:20010/v1
#     API Key:   same value as ASK_HTTP_TOKEN
#     Model:     jarvis   (placeholder — the shim ignores the model id)
#   Smoke test from the HA host:
#     curl -s http://<mini-pc-ip>:20010/health
#     curl -s http://<mini-pc-ip>:20010/v1/chat/completions \
#       -H "Authorization: Bearer $ASK_HTTP_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#       -d '{"model":"jarvis","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"What is the current date?"}]}'
# ASK_HTTP_TOKEN=generate-a-long-random-token-here
# ASK_HTTP_PORT=20010
# ASK_HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1
# For HA Assist on another host, uncomment:
# ASK_HTTP_BIND=0.0.0.0

# Alexa custom skill (talk to JARVIS via an Echo Dot) — POST /alexa on the same
# HTTP server as /ask (see telegram-bot/src/alexa.js and alexa/README.md).
# The Echo does its own speech-to-text / text-to-speech; this bridges the
# transcribed utterance to askCore and speaks the reply. Auth is the Alexa
# request SIGNATURE (verified server-side) + your skill id — NOT the bearer
# token. Setting ALEXA_SKILL_ID is enough to enable /alexa (ASK_HTTP_TOKEN is
# not required; if unset the token-protected /ask + /v1 routes simply 401).
#
# Alexa gives a skill only ~8s to answer, so fast asks (weather, HA, a quick
# lookup) are spoken inline; slow/complex asks (cross-provider MCP, delegate)
# ack "Right away, Sir" and JARVIS then speaks the finished answer on the Echo
# via Home Assistant's notify.alexa_media (reuses HA_URL / HA_TOKEN above).
#
# ALEXA_SKILL_ID:         your skill's applicationId (amzn1.ask.skill.…). Required.
# ALEXA_NOTIFY_SERVICE:   HA notify service for delayed answers
#                         (default alexa_media_alines_echo_dot — matches this house).
# ALEXA_NOTIFY_TYPE:      alexa_media data.type — tts | announce | push (default tts).
# ALEXA_NOTIFY_TARGET:    optional CSV of media_player.* targets (only for the
#                         generic notify.alexa_media service; omit if the notify
#                         service already targets a single Echo).
# ALEXA_INLINE_BUDGET_MS: wait this long for an inline answer before falling back
#                         to announce (default 6000; keep it under Alexa's ~8s).
# ALEXA_VERIFY_SIGNATURE: set to "false" ONLY for local curl testing (default true).
# ALEXA_SKILL_ID=amzn1.ask.skill.xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx
# ALEXA_NOTIFY_SERVICE=alexa_media_alines_echo_dot
# ALEXA_NOTIFY_TYPE=tts
# ALEXA_INLINE_BUDGET_MS=6000

# Self-development (JARVIS edits its own code) — POWERFUL, opt-in.
# When enabled, "change your own code / add a feature to yourself / make X
# self-healing" requests let JARVIS edit the jarvis-home/ source in the repo
# checkout, syntax-check it, commit, and push to the CURRENT branch. Deploying
# (redeploy + self-restart) is always a separate one-tap confirmation in Telegram.
# SELFDEV_ENABLED: master switch (default false). Set to true to allow it.
# JARVIS_REPO_DIR: path to the git checkout (default ~/repos/jarvis-ui).
# SELFDEV_MODEL:   model used for the code edit (default claude-opus-5).
# GIT_PUSH_TOKEN:  optional GitHub PAT (contents:write, this repo) used only to
#   push. Omit if the server already has push credentials (SSH key / cached https).
# NOTE: the repo must be writable by the bot's user (chown it to yourself and
#   drop the `sudo` from the jarvis-update alias) for commits/pushes to work.
SELFDEV_ENABLED=false
# JARVIS_REPO_DIR=/home/iot/repos/jarvis-ui
# SELFDEV_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# GIT_PUSH_TOKEN=github_pat_xxx

# Daily morning briefing
# BRIEFING_ENABLED: set to "false" to disable the scheduled briefing (default: enabled)
# BRIEFING_TIME: 24h HH:MM in Jerusalem local time (default: 07:00)
# WEATHER_ENTITY: optional HA weather entity id (auto-detected if omitted, e.g. weather.forecast_home)
BRIEFING_ENABLED=true
BRIEFING_TIME=07:00

# USB webcam (ustreamer host service + /cam command + away-mode snapshots).
# Set WEBCAM_ENABLED=false to skip webcam setup in setup.sh (step 13b). Handy
# when there's no camera attached or it's misbehaving — setup then completes
# cleanly instead of aborting. To fully stop a running feed on the host:
#   systemctl --user stop jarvis-ustreamer.service
#   systemctl --user mask jarvis-ustreamer.service   # blocks the watchdog from reviving it
# (unmask + WEBCAM_ENABLED=true to re-enable later.)
# WEBCAM_ENABLED=true
# WEATHER_ENTITY=weather.forecast_home
# Briefing sections (all default on; set to false to hide). Each is also
# skipped automatically if the underlying HA data isn't available.
# JEWISH_ENABLED=true        # Hebrew date, parsha, Omer, Shabbat times (jewish_calendar integration)
# GARMIN_ENABLED=true        # body battery, sleep, RHR, steps, training, stress (Garmin Connect)
# GARMIN_PREFIX=             # optional, auto-detected (e.g. sensor.garmin_connect_amir_)
# CALENDAR_ENABLED=true      # today's events from HA calendar entities
# CALENDAR_ENTITIES=         # optional CSV override (auto-detects all calendar.* except hebcal)
